llest one  which actually isn't depicted here has  four genes like i mentioned  um before most of  all of the reference habla types or most  of them anyway in the human  genome reference were um submitted um in  sequence by  dan garrity's lab at the university of  washington and cisco genomics  and what he does is he uses phosmine  library preparation to separate  the maternal and paternal chromosomes  and then sequence them  pac bio and because they use library  preparation they can get read links of  tens of thousands of base pairs  and that's how they were able to  contribute these to the human genome  reference  and this this is what we use as the  ground truth for our project  but our goal was to use high throughput  next generation  sequencing methods to develop a full  haplotype  system a system that labs can use  that don't even have to know anything  about cure in order to efficiently  sequence and assemble  the primary barrier to doing this in the  past at the high throughput ngs  workflow is the homology and  this dot plot shows the alignment  between a haplotype on the y curve on  the y axis  and a b haplotype on the x axis  and every red dot is a match between the  two haplotypes  and you can see that not only are the  two hepatites in general  homologous but every gene is homologous  to every other gene  and this is what causes the most  problems for  interpretation for example if you try  and  align reads to a cure reference you're  going to get reads that multi-align  some reads will align to almost every  gene in the alignment  and as a bioinformatics person i  this is a problem and it's frustrating  but the key innovation that we have for  our new library preparation is we want  to take advantage of that homology  if we have to pay the price for it on  the bioinformatics interpretation  let's take advantage of it from the  capture and  capture use the homology to capture  multiple  regions with just one probe  and so that's how we went about  designing our probes we partnered with  idt  and they gave us a candidate senate we  gave them some cure sequences  they gave us a candidate set of probes  and then i used the domain knowledge  that  i that i have for here to optimize those  probe sets  this picture shows a hapless a b  haplotype that's almost 200 000 base  pairs long  the the vertical bars at the top are the  where the genes are annotated  and then in the middle it shows we zoom  into a  region that's approximately 50 000 base  pairs  and we show the alignment of the 120  base pair capture probes  against this haplotype  with our capture method the  fragment lengths are about 5 000 base  pairs  on average so we needed to have capture  probes um  less than that um every 4 4 500 is what  we  ended up with and  the yellow arrows show the order of the  probes each probe has a number  so from left to right the number starts  off 4 3 12  10 2 etc  and then below those probes um at the  very bottom  the blue horizontal bars are annotation  of the transposable and repetitive  elements  that are in the haplotype and also the  exons  it was important to have our probes miss  those regions because  some of the pure exons are very  polymorphic and that polymorphism might  cause the probes not to be able to hit  them and  the repetitive elements it's important  to avoid those so we don't  get off cure reads fragments  and the thing i'd like to point out next  is  the order of the probes repeats so it  starts off 4  3 12 10 but then about 10  000 base pairs later it also repeats 4  3 12 no 10 this time and then  10 000 base pairs later approximately  repeats again  4 3 12 10 and this shows that  since these probes are hitting in  multiple spots along the chromosome  we can also capture them in multiple and  the same probe can also  capture multiple parts of the chromosome  and that's where the novelty and  efficiency comes in  so to go through the workflow at a high  level  first we do the capture and sequencing  this  the circle shows the fragments and then  the below that is the  four probes that are um binding with the  those fragments and capturing them and  then we error correct the reeds  even though the pack biohi-fi reads are  very very accurate  we still find it's beneficial to go  through an error correction step  and then we go through a binning step  where we've been the reads  first we've been on and off here that's  the big circle on the right  but we also use the order of the probe  motifs to the extent we can we also bin  the reeds per  gene and then we assemble each of those  bins with canoe  from the assemblers point of view it's  just de novo sequencing it's just given  the sequences in the band and de novo  sequence them or  assemble them in a diploid way  although we use um canoe and it seems to  be working well for us  any assembler can be swapped in  at that point then comes the gene  annotation step and again here we used  to order the gene probes to  locate the location to precisely  annotate the location of the genes in  each haplotype  and then using other methods that i'll  explain later we also  annotate the location of exons within  the gene  within each gene and this slide shows  the res  the results of our assembly tests  compared to ground truth and  we put we use two populations for our  validation  one is a european population and one is  the african-american  population  and the column two shows the different  experiments  that varied by the number of probes that  we used first we used  the initial candidate set of 200 probes  on the european  um set i should say each population has  eight individuals  16 haplotypes so all total we had 32  haplotypes that we evaluated  in these two populations um so we  started out with 200 probes  and then we went to optimize that 15  probes for the europeans  and then we tried that 15 probes that  again for the african americans  and then we had a final optimized set of  18 probes on the african americans  