s is  the recombination in the hot spot  the particularly strong recombination  hotspot in the middle of the haplotype  it's about 10 000 base pairs long and  if we zoom in to that 10 000 base pairs  in the bottom  this depicts a an alignment speak of  that  region to itself and so the  xx it's cut off at the top because the  important thing is at the bottom the red  diagonal line from lower left to upper  right that's just an identity line that  shows that  it matches itself but the other lines to  the right side  show where the where the haplotype  sequence um  aligns to itself but in a different  locus in a different location  thousands thousands of base pairs away  and the blue indicates it matches itself  in the reverse  orientation and the red indicates that  it uh  it's in the same forward orientation  at the very bottom i've annotated the  transposable elements that are here  and i highlighted the the alu elements  in yellow each one of those alu elements  has  a long l1 uh line element on either side  and that combination of the aluminum  line is where the recombination  spot is so you can see that the reason  this recombination hot spot is  so particularly strong is that it has  this the  at least three and a row of these  recombination hot spots two in one  direction  and one in the other and um hopefully  you can  appreciate why um this was the most  difficult  traditionally anyway the most difficult  parts of these haplotypes  to assemble  going and going back to the dot plot of  the a haplotype with the b  hepatite it's probably hard to see but  each blue box in this picture  is that same um blue  that the blue and the recombination  alignment that we just saw and so  hopefully you can see that there's  little blue boxes  spread throughout the haplotypes and  that's why that's what causes the  all these variations in the structures  and why recombination can happen almost  anywhere  in these haplotypes this isn't a problem  just for assembly it's a problem for  alignment too  and now um we i'm switching gears to  another project where we created the  first multiple sequence alignment of all  cure haplotypes  it wasn't possible before because we had  we have a situation where we have  um almost 70 um cure references now  each between 70 and 270 000 base pairs  long  most aligners can't handle that size and  the ones that can get thrown off by hom  topology so we were we piggybacked on  our full haplotype assembly approach to  create  a full haplotype multiple sequence  alignment of all care haplotypes  and the way we started was going back to  the motifs of these  capture probes if you look at the yellow  arrows  this time i annotated each combination  of probes  with a letter so from four to three is c  from three to twelves i and so on and in  this way we can  summarize each haplotype as a order  direct  order of those probes and we can  summarize each haplotype instead of  hundreds of thousands of ac's g's and  t's  we can some right summarize each happen  type in about 100 characters  and with that 100 characters we can also  easily create a multiple sequence  alignment just  like you would for any other characters  and so that's what we did here  this represents a multiple sequence  alignment of the cure haplotypes  to a resolution of within 5 000 base  pairs  then we use that high level um  resolution habits  well first of all we translate it we  transpose that  alignment into a tree and on the right i  annotated the  names of the haplotypes with their human  annotation  and if i draw the cutoff line in the  tree  right where the red line red vertical  line is  then the branches that are annotated  at that level perfectly match the  previous human annotations of those  haplotypes  thereby demonstrating that these motifs  reconstruct the human annotation  of these haplotypes and so then using  that high level annotation we can micro  we can  might we can micro align the dna  at the single base pair and get a 